From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Make the semaphore spin count a macro

moodycamel's LightweightSemaphore spins up to 10,000 times before it blocks in
the kernel. That is the default for every guest thread's `running` and
`initialized` semaphores, and every guest handoff goes through them. On one
processor the thread that would end the spin by signalling cannot run while
this one spins. The spin can only succeed if the signal has already arrived,
and the first check sees that.

MOODYCAMEL_LIGHTWEIGHT_SEMAPHORE_SPINS replaces the constant. It defaults to
10,000, so nothing changes unless a target sets it. The Windows 95 target sets
it to 0 in cmake/win95-target.cmake. Normal mode, trace off, 150 s:

  10,000 spins   97.4  98.2  98.8 ms
  0 spins        96.3  96.4 ms

All of it comes off `elsewhere`, the guest side: 82.1-82.5 ms to 80.2-80.4.

---
--- a/thirdparty/concurrentqueue/lightweightsemaphore.h
+++ b/thirdparty/concurrentqueue/lightweightsemaphore.h
@@ -346,7 +346,14 @@
 	}
 
 public:
-	LightweightSemaphore(ssize_t initialCount = 0, int maxSpins = 10000) : m_count(initialCount), m_maxSpins(maxSpins)
+	// DKR-R, patch 0053: the spin count is a macro so that a one-processor target
+	// can set it to zero. On one processor, the thread that would end the spin
+	// by signalling cannot run while this one spins, so the spin can only
+	// succeed if the signal has already arrived -- which the first check sees.
+#ifndef MOODYCAMEL_LIGHTWEIGHT_SEMAPHORE_SPINS
+#define MOODYCAMEL_LIGHTWEIGHT_SEMAPHORE_SPINS 10000
+#endif
+	LightweightSemaphore(ssize_t initialCount = 0, int maxSpins = MOODYCAMEL_LIGHTWEIGHT_SEMAPHORE_SPINS) : m_count(initialCount), m_maxSpins(maxSpins)
 	{
 		assert(initialCount >= 0);
 		assert(maxSpins >= 0);
